        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Found it — and it's a real bug my sessionizer rewrite could trigger. Fixed now.\n\n## Root cause\n\nYour tmux pane's shell was started with `PWD=/home/himmel/dev/environment/` — **trailing slash** (confirmed in `/proc/<zsh-pid>/environ`). Two facts combine:\n\n1. zsh keeps an inherited slashed `$PWD` verbatim instead of normalizing it.\n2. robbyrussell shows the directory via `%c` (trailing path component), and with a trailing slash `%c` expands to **empty** — I reproduced this deterministically (`env PWD=/tmp/x/sub/ zsh` → prompt `➜ git:(…)` with no directory, while a normal shell shows it).\n\nWhere did the slash come from? `fd` appends `/` to directories. My rewritten sessionizer switched discovery to `fd` without stripping that, so a selected path like `…/environment/` went straight into `tmux new-session -c …` and poisoned the pane's `$PWD`. (Your `~/scripts` is a symlink into the repo, so the new code went live the moment I saved it — matches your fresh `environment` session from 21:25.)\n\n## Fixes (2 files)\n\n- `home/scripts/tmux-sessionizer` — strip trailing slashes from the selected path right after fzf/argv selection, restoring parity with the old `find` output.\n- `home/.zshrc` — normalize a slashed `$PWD` at startup (`cd \"${PWD%/}\"`), so every entry vector is covered: tmux `-c`, terminal `--dir` flags, ssh, nested shells. Root `/` is excluded.\n\n## For your current broken pane\n\nJust run `cd .` + Enter in it (normalizes `$PWD`), or open a fresh window. The prompt comes back as `➜ environment git:(main)`.\n\n## Verified\n\n- `bash -n` (sessionizer) and `zsh -n` (.zshrc) clean.\n- Full-zshrc test: `env PWD=/tmp/slashtest/sub/ zsh -i` → `PWD=[/tmp/slashtest/sub] DIR=[sub]`.\n- End-to-end on a scratch socket with slashed input `\"$PROJ/\"`: session created, pane environ shows slash-free `PWD=…/myproj`.\n- All scratch tmux sockets and `/tmp` test files cleaned up; live `environment` session untouched (read-only inspection only).\n\nOne honesty note: your screenshot's prompt also seemed to lack the cyan color escape around the (empty) directory. I chased that extensively — prompt engine, terminfo, and `colors` are all healthy (a `zsh -f` control renders cyan fine with slashed PWD). Most likely it's stale bytes from that long-idle pane's original draw; once the directory renders again after `cd .`, the color comes with it since it's a literal in the expanded prompt. If cyan is still missing after that, tell me and I'll dig further.",
